course: dictionary
lesson: 1041
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words look the same but mean different things. «Bore» is one of them. The words around it, the context, tell you which meaning is right.
explain: «Kieran gets bored of it within a week.» Here bored means he has lost interest because the game is dull. It describes how he feels, not what he is like.
explain: «What a bore, the bus is late» uses bore as a name for a nuisance, something that annoys you. Read the whole sentence to see which meaning fits.
explain: «The tree bore fruit every autumn» uses bore as the past of bear, which means carried or produced. Try swapping in another word, such as «produced», and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Bore can be a verb, a noun, or the past tense of bear.
fact: «Bored» means feeling tired and uninterested because something is dull.
fact: «A bore» can mean a dull person or thing, as in «He's such a bore».
fact: To bore means to drill a deep round hole, and a borehole is a hole drilled to find water.
fact: A tidal bore is a big wave that rushes up some rivers, like the Severn Bore in England.
exercise: «Kieran gets bored of it within a week.» What does bored mean here? | *Tired and uninterested because something is dull | Sad and crying about a loss | Excited and rushing about | Hungry and cold
exercise: In design and technology, a pupil must bore a hole in the wood. What does bore mean? | To wave goodbye to a friend | To read a book all afternoon | *To drill a deep round hole | To carry something heavy up the stairs
exercise: «The tree bore fruit every autumn.» What does bore mean here? | Was dull and tiresome | Made a loud noise in the wind | Drilled a hole in the trunk | *Produced or carried, as the past of bear
test: Which meaning fits «What a bore, the bus is late»? | Carried something heavy | A deep round hole | A big wave in a river | *A nuisance, something that annoys you
test: «Not reacting can make a teasing game boring.» What does boring mean here? | *Dull and tiresome, not fun | Very exciting and loud | Full of water | Carried by someone strong
exam: Which of these is a tidal bore? | The past of the word bear, as in «she bore the pain» | *A big wave that rushes up some rivers, like the Severn Bore | A dull person who talks too much | A drill used to make holes in wood
